    "Input sanitizer on every user turn: NFKC + zero-width removal, 2000-char cap, XML escape, removal of whole forged e-mail/source/excerpt blocks, neutralized ignore/roleplay phrases (addresses the Lab 6.1 roleplay and fake-block probes); a turn with no real question left is rejected without an LLM call",
    "XML trust boundary: retrieved PDF chunks are XML-escaped inside <documents>, the question sits in <user_question>, and HARDENED_XML_SYSTEM says paper text is data and must never be obeyed (addresses forged tags and poisoned passages)",
    "Planner sub-queries are model output feeding the retriever, so they are sanitized, capped at 2 per step and 300 characters; the loop is capped at MAX_STEPS and every retrieval is written to an action log",
    "Corpus scan for instruction-like passages, counted and logged for monitoring (never silently deleted)"
  ],
  "cost_optimizations": [
    "Cache: normalized exact match plus a fuzzy token-overlap match (Jaccard >= 0.8) with a guard that refuses a hit when numbers or direction words (before/after/not/...) differ",
    "Router: single-call direct path for one-hop questions; planner loop only for multipart, comparison and trend questions",
    "Model mix: gpt-5.4-nano plans, gpt-5.4-mini writes the grounded answer",
    "Retrieval adjustment: BM25 over ~900-character chunks of the full text of all papers, at most 2 chunks per paper and 4 per query, instead of the first 20,000 characters of several whole papers"
  ],
  "measurable_gains": [
    "Tokens, LLM calls, latency and USD cost on the 9-question benchmark, before (6.1 behaviour) vs after (cold) vs warm cache",
    "Cache hit: tokens and latency for a repeat question, and hits among 3 paraphrases",
    "Probe flags per condition (baseline, xml_only, full) over 7 probes x 2 trials, with outputs printed",
    "Quality guards: expected-term match, expected-paper retrieval rate, quote support against the corpus"
  ],
  "residual_risks": [
    "Staged multi-turn attacks are not tested here; monitor with per-session action logs and a turn-count review",
    "A fuzzy cache can still serve a stale answer to a context-dependent paraphrase the guard words do not cover; keep a short TTL and track the cache hit rate against user corrections",
    "Regex sanitizer false positives ('act as a verifier', 'from now on') alter legitimate questions and novel phrasings can evade it; track the removal rate per day and review a sample of altered turns",
    "Keyword/BM25 retrieval misses paraphrased concepts, and quoted spans can be broken by PDF hyphenation, so the quote-support check understates grounding"
  ]
